tel 1.0

base panopticon
import soundness

info   lenses and optics: deep updates to immutable values, written as a path and an assignment
detail
    _Panopticon_ updates a value deep inside an immutable structure without the nested `copy`
    calls which would otherwise rebuild every layer above it. The `lens` method takes updates
    written as plain path assignments — `_.ceo.name = t"Bill"` — and returns the new value,
    rebuilding only the structure on the paths it touches. A step in parentheses traverses a
    collection: an `Ordinal` picks one element, `Each` reaches every element, a key indexes a
    `Map`, and a `Filter` selects by a predicate.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Role(name: Text, count: Int)
    case class Person(name: Text, roles: List[Role])
    case class Company(ceo: Person, name: Text)

    val company = Company(Person(t"John", List(Role(t"CEO", 1), Role(t"CFO", 2))), t"Acme")

    company.lens(_.ceo.name = t"Bill")
    company.lens(_.ceo.roles(Each).count = 0, _.name = t"Acme Ltd")
    company.lens(_.ceo.roles(Filter[Role](_.count > 1)).name = t"$prior!")
    ```

    Underneath the syntax are ordinary values. A `Lens` is a getter and a setter, and an
    `Optic` the more general form which can only modify — one element, many, or none. A lens
    onto any case-class field is summoned as `"field" is Lens from Type`, generated by the
    compiler from the type's structure (`Optic.deref`); an `Optical` turns a traversal value
    such as `Each` or an ordinal into an optic over a particular collection; and two lenses or
    two optics `compose` into one which reaches all the way through.

    The same syntax updates the dynamic data formats. Each format supplies a lens and opticals
    over its own value type in its `optics` package — `jsonLens` and `jsonEachOptical` for
    JSON, `xmlLens` for XML, `telLens` for TEL, `dsvCellLens` and `dsvRowOptical` for CSV and
    DSV — and those givens are imported by name, as `import soundness.*` does not import
    givens. The format's `conversions` package (`encodableToJson`, `encodableToTel`, …) lets a
    bare encodable value stand on the right of the `=`, through `Coercible`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import dynamicAccess.dynamicJson, conversions.encodableToJson
    import optics.{jsonLens, jsonEachOptical}

    case class Role(name: Text)
    case class Person(name: Text, roles: List[Role])
    case class Company(ceo: Person)

    val json = Company(Person(t"John", List(Role(t"CEO")))).in[Json]
    json.lens(_.ceo.name = t"Bill", _.ceo.roles(Each).name = t"member")
    ```

    Updates sharing a prefix are fused by the `lens` macro, so the shared structure is
    rebuilt once; a path the macro cannot analyse (a `Filter` traversal, whose operand is not
    a singleton) falls back to applying the updates one after another, with the same result.
    Later updates in a call see the results of earlier ones: of two writes to one field the
    later wins, and a write inside a field after a write of the whole field refines it.

    The library is one source set, `panopticon.core`, and everything is exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella.

refer panopticon.lens
refer panopticon.Lens
refer panopticon.Optic
refer panopticon.Optical
refer panopticon.compose

entry .lens   applies updates written as path assignments to a value, returning the new value
    `value.lens(update, …)` takes one or more lambdas, each a path from the value ending in an
    assignment, and applies them in order:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Role(name: Text, count: Int)
    case class Person(name: Text, roles: List[Role])
    case class Company(ceo: Person, name: Text)

    val company = Company(Person(t"John", List(Role(t"CEO", 1), Role(t"CFO", 2))), t"Acme")

    company.lens(_.ceo.name = t"Bill")                    // one field
    company.lens(_.ceo.roles(Prim).name = t"Founder")     // the first role
    company.lens(_.ceo.roles(Each).count = 0)             // every role
    company.lens(_.ceo.roles(Each).name = t"$prior!")     // every role, from its old name
    company.lens(_.ceo.name = t"Bill", _.name = t"Bill & Co")
    ```

    A field step needs a `Lens` for that name and type: for a case class, `Optic.deref`
    generates one, and a format supplies one in its `optics` package. A step in parentheses
    needs an `Optical` for the traversal and the collection: `Ordinal`s over `List` and
    `Sequence`, a key or a key `Filter` over a `Map`, `Each` and an element `Filter` over a
    `List`, and whatever a format defines. Within an assignment, `prior` is the value being
    replaced. The right-hand side may be of any type `Coercible` to the target: its own type
    or a subtype, or one with a `Conversion` in scope.

    The macro groups updates which share a path prefix, so the structure along that prefix is
    rebuilt once however many fields beneath it change; where a step is not a field or a
    singleton traversal (a `Filter`), it applies each update in turn instead. A path naming a
    field the type does not have is a compile error, _`Type` has no field called `name`_.

entry .compose   composes two lenses, or two optics, into one which reaches through both
    `left.compose(right)` joins a lens from `A` onto `B` with one from `B` onto `C`, giving a
    lens from `A` onto `C` which reads by reading both and writes by rebuilding both; two
    optics compose into an optic likewise. It needs a `Composable` for the pair, which the
    `Composable` companion provides for lenses and for optics. The result captures whatever
    the two parts capture, so composing a fallible lens gives a fallible one.

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label A lens onto a company's CEO
      code
          import soundness.*

          case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
          case class Company(ceo: Person, name: Text)

          val company = Company(Person(t"John", 52), t"Acme")
          val ceo: Lens from Company onto Person = summon["ceo" is Lens from Company]

          ceo(company)   // Person(t"John", 52)
    frame
      label Composed with a lens onto a person's name
      code
          import soundness.*

          case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
          case class Company(ceo: Person, name: Text)

          val company = Company(Person(t"John", 52), t"Acme")
          val ceo: Lens from Company onto Person = summon["ceo" is Lens from Company]
          val name: Lens from Person onto Text = summon["name" is Lens from Person]
          val ceoName = ceo.compose(name)

          ceoName(company)   // t"John"
    frame
      label Applied to write the CEO's name
      code
          import soundness.*

          case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
          case class Company(ceo: Person, name: Text)

          val company = Company(Person(t"John", 52), t"Acme")
          val ceo: Lens from Company onto Person = summon["ceo" is Lens from Company]
          val name: Lens from Person onto Text = summon["name" is Lens from Person]
          val ceoName = ceo.compose(name)

          ceoName(company) = t"Bill"   // Company(Person(t"Bill", 52), t"Acme")
    frame
      label Applied to modify it
      code
          import soundness.*

          case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
          case class Company(ceo: Person, name: Text)

          val company = Company(Person(t"John", 52), t"Acme")
          val ceo: Lens from Company onto Person = summon["ceo" is Lens from Company]
          val name: Lens from Person onto Text = summon["name" is Lens from Person]
          val ceoName = ceo.compose(name)

          ceoName.modify(company)(_.upper)   // Company(Person(t"JOHN", 52), t"Acme")
    ##
    ```

    Where the whole path is known in one place, `lens` says it more directly; composition is
    for paths assembled from parts held separately.

entry .Optic   a way to modify the part or parts of a value it focuses on: the general form of a lens
  detail
      `Optic from Origin onto Target` can rebuild an `Origin` by transforming the `Target`s it
      focuses on, with `#modify`. Unlike a `Lens` it need not focus on exactly one target: an
      optic over a list's elements focuses on all of them, and one over a missing key on none,
      so it can modify but not read. Every `Lens` is an `Optic`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val first = summon[Prim.type is Optic from List[Int] onto Int]
      first.modify(List(1, 2, 3))(_*10)   // List(10, 2, 3)
      ```

      Optics extend themselves dynamically, which is how a `lens` path is typed: `optic.name`
      continues through the field lens for `name`, `optic(traversal)` through the `Optical`
      for the traversal, and `optic.name = value` or `optic(traversal) = value` ends the path
      in an assignment, giving the function from `Origin` to `Origin` which performs it. The
      `lens` method begins each path from `Optic.identity`.

      An `Optic` is capture-tracked (`Findable`): one built over a fallible transform, such as
      a format's lens which needs a `Tactic`, captures it, and so does any optic composed
      from it.
  refer panopticon.Lens
  refer panopticon.Optical
  refer panopticon.lens
  entry .deref   the lens onto a case-class field, generated by name: `"name" is Lens from Type`
      A transparent inline given, so `summon["name" is Lens from Person]` is a lens whose
      `Target` is the field's precise type. It reads the field and writes it through the
      companion's `apply` with the other fields unchanged. It applies to any `Product` type;
      a name the type has no field for is a compile error, _`Person` has no field called
      `nmae`_. The given cannot be expanded inside a quoted expression: keep such a summons in
      an ordinary method.
  entry .identity   the optic which focuses on the whole value: the start of every `lens` path
  entry .apply   an optic from its modification function: how the origin is rebuilt from a target transform
      `Optic[Self, Origin, Target]((origin, transform) => …)` builds an optic whose `modify`
      calls the function. The result captures whatever the function captures, so an optic over
      a transform which needs a `Tactic` carries that need to where it is used. This is how an
      `Optical` given makes its optics.
  entry .prim   the optic onto a list's first element, for the traversal `Prim`
      Modifies the head of a list and leaves the rest; an empty list is unchanged.
  entry #Self     the type the optic is keyed by: a field name's literal type, or a traversal's type
  entry #Origin   the type of value the optic modifies
  entry #Target   the type of the part or parts the optic focuses on
  entry #modify   rebuilds a value by transforming the target or targets the optic focuses on
      `optic.modify(origin)(transform)` applies the transform to each focused target and
      returns the rebuilt origin; a target which is not there (a missing key, an ordinal past
      the end) leaves the origin unchanged.
  entry #selectDynamic   `optic.name`: the optic continued through the lens for the field `name`
  entry #updateDynamic   `optic.name = value`: the function which sets the field `name` at the optic's focus
      The value may refer to `prior`, the value it replaces, and is coerced to the field's type
      by a `Coercible` given. This is the shape each assignment in a `lens` call takes.
  entry #update   `optic(traversal) = value`: the function which sets every target the traversal reaches
      Needs an `Optical` for the traversal over the optic's `Target`, and the value to be
      `Coercible` to the traversal's target type.
  entry #applyDynamic   `optic.name(traversal)`: the optic continued through a field, then a traversal of it
  entry #apply   `optic(traversal)`: the optic continued through a traversal, such as `Each` or an ordinal
      Needs an `Optical` for the traversal's type over the optic's `Target`.

entry .Lens   a getter and a setter for one part of a value: an optic which can also read
  detail
      `Lens from Origin onto Target` reads the target out of an origin with `#apply` and
      writes one back with `#update`, returning a new origin. A lens onto a case-class field is
      generated by name (`Optic.deref`) and a format provides one for its own fields; any other
      is built from its two functions with `Lens(get, set)`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      val name = summon["name" is Lens from Person]
      val person = Person(t"Ada", 36)

      name(person)               // t"Ada"
      name(person) = t"Grace"    // Person(t"Grace", 36)
      name.modify(person)(_.upper)   // Person(t"ADA", 36)
      ```

      Two lenses `compose` into a lens through both.
  refer panopticon.Optic
  refer panopticon.compose
  entry .apply   a lens from a getter and a setter
      `Lens[Self, Origin, Target](get, set)`: `get` reads the target from an origin, and `set`
      takes an origin and a new target and returns the rebuilt origin. The lens captures
      whatever the two functions capture, so one whose accessors close over a `Tactic` is a
      capturing lens and the requirement reaches its use site.
  entry #apply    reads the target out of a value
  entry #update   a copy of a value with the target replaced: `lens(origin) = target`
  entry #modify   a copy of a value with the target transformed: read, apply the function, write back

entry .Optical   turns a traversal value, such as `Each` or an ordinal, into an optic over a collection
  detail
      `Traversal is Optical from Origin onto Target` makes, from a value of the traversal type,
      an `Optic from Origin onto Target`. It is what gives meaning to a step in parentheses in
      a `lens` path: `_.roles(Each)` uses the `Optical` for `Each.type` over the type of
      `roles`. The companion provides:

      - `ordinalList` and `ordinalSeries`: an `Ordinal` picks one element of a `List` or a
        `Sequence`, which is unchanged if the ordinal is past its end;
      - `at`: a key picks one value of a `Map`, which is unchanged if the key is absent;
      - `filter`: a `Filter` over keys picks every value of a `Map` whose key matches;
      - `filter2`: a `Filter` over elements picks every element of a `List` which matches.

      `Each` has its own in its companion, and a format provides its opticals in its `optics`
      package, to import by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Role(name: Text, count: Int)
      case class User(name: Text, roles: Map[Text, Role])

      val user = User(t"Ada", Map(t"ceo" -> Role(t"CEO", 1), t"cfo" -> Role(t"CFO", 2)))
      user.lens(_.roles(t"cfo").count = 3)
      user.lens(_.roles(Filter[Text](_ != t"ceo")).name = t"$prior (acting)")
      ```
  refer panopticon.Each
  refer panopticon.Filter
  entry .ordinalList     an `Ordinal` as the optic onto one element of a `List`
  entry .ordinalSeries   an `Ordinal` as the optic onto one element of a `Sequence`
  entry .at              a key as the optic onto its value in a `Map`; an absent key leaves the map unchanged
  entry .filter          a `Filter` over keys as the optic onto every value of a `Map` whose key matches
  entry .filter2         a `Filter` over elements as the optic onto every element of a `List` which matches
  entry #Self     the traversal type
  entry #Target   the type of the parts the optic focuses on
  entry #Origin   the type of collection the optic modifies
  entry #optic    the optic for a value of the traversal type

entry .Each   the traversal which reaches every element of a collection
  detail
      `_.roles(Each)` in a `lens` path focuses on every element of `roles`, so one assignment
      updates them all: `company.lens(_.ceo.roles(Each).count = 0)`. The companion's given
      covers `List`; formats provide their own for their arrays (`jsonEachOptical`,
      `xmlEachOptical`, `telEachOptical`, `dsvRowEachOptical`).
  refer panopticon.Optical
  entry .optical   `Each` as the optic onto every element of a `List`

entry .Filter   a traversal which reaches the elements, or the map values, whose element or key matches a predicate
  detail
      `Filter[Role](_.count > 1)` in a `lens` path focuses on the elements of a list which
      satisfy the predicate; `Filter[Text](_ == t"cfo")` over a `Map` focuses on the values
      whose key does. The predicate is a pure function: a filter is part of an optic, which is
      a pure value, so the predicate may not capture a capability or mutable state.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Role(name: Text, count: Int)
      case class Person(name: Text, roles: List[Role])

      val person = Person(t"John", List(Role(t"CEO", 1), Role(t"CFO", 2), Role(t"CIO", 3)))
      person.lens(_.roles(Filter[Role](_.count > 1)).count = 0)
      // Person(t"John", List(Role(t"CEO", 1), Role(t"CFO", 0), Role(t"CIO", 0)))
      ```

      A `lens` call with a `Filter` step cannot be fused, since its operand is not a singleton,
      so its updates are applied one at a time; the result is the same.
  refer panopticon.Optical
  entry #predicate   the test an element, or a map key, must pass to be focused on

entry .Coercible   evidence that a value may be assigned where a lens's target type is expected
  detail
      `Source is Coercible to Target` converts the right-hand side of an assignment in a `lens`
      path to the type of what it replaces. A value of the target type, or a subtype, needs no
      coercion (`identity`, in `Coercible2`, at lower priority); a value with a `Conversion`
      to the target in scope is converted (`conversion`). The latter is how a format's
      `conversions` package — `encodableToJson`, `encodableToYaml`, … — lets a bare `Text` or
      case class be assigned into a document, and how gossamer's conversion lets a string
      literal be assigned to a `Text` field. An assignment with no `Coercible` is a compile
      error, _cannot coerce `Source` to `Target` in this assignment_.
  refer panopticon.lens
  entry .conversion   coerces any value with a `Conversion` to the target type in scope
  entry #Self     the type of the value being assigned
  entry #Result   the target type it is assigned to
  entry #coerce   converts the assigned value to the target type

entry .Coercible2   the lower-priority `Coercible` given, for a value already of the target type
  detail
      Mixed into the `Coercible` companion so that a `Conversion`, where there is one, is
      tried first.
  entry #identity   a value of the target type, or of a subtype, assigned as it is

entry .Composable   how two lenses or optics compose into one which reaches through both
  detail
      `Left is Composable by Right to Result` is the typeclass `compose` needs. The companion
      gives one for two lenses, giving a lens, and one for two optics, giving an optic; the
      composite captures whatever its parts capture.
  refer panopticon.compose
  entry .optics   composes an optic from `A` onto `B` with one from `B` onto `C`
  entry .lenses   composes a lens from `A` onto `B` with one from `B` onto `C`, giving a lens which reads and writes
  entry #Self          the left-hand type
  entry #Operand       the right-hand type
  entry #Result        the composite's type
  entry #composition   the composite of a left and a right value, capturing both

entry .internal   the macros behind `lens` and `Optic.deref`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home.
  entry .lens   the macro behind `Optic.deref`: the lens onto a case-class field, by name
  entry .fuse   the macro behind `lens`: groups updates by shared prefix and rebuilds each prefix once
